CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: Paul orders the gathered Body by separate instructions, and each instruction gives its own ground. It has tried to show that from his sentences. The Body is one and has many members, and members are set in it as God pleased. The ascended Christ gave gifts for the work of the ministry and the edifying of the body. Paul names two offices in the local assembly, the overseer, who is also called the elder, and the deacon, and he gives the qualifications of each. In the meeting, God is not the author of confusion, and all things are to be done decently and in order. For men and women he gives instructions whose grounds are headship, creation and the law, and the volume has argued that those grounds are not local custom. In discipline he gives aims: purity, a spirit saved, shame, restoration and warning."),
("p", "The instructions share a subject and a few words, and they differ in the question each answers. The volume has asked of each the same four questions, and has not carried the ground of one into another. It has also said where each passage stops. Paul does not say how many elders a church must have, how they are chosen beyond his own appointing, what a meeting must look like in every detail, or what a church must do in cases he does not name. The volume has not supplied these."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What is the assembly?", "The church of God in a place, the Body of Christ at that place", "1Cor 1:2; 12:27"),
    ("Why are there many members?", "The Body is one and has many members, set as God pleased", "1Cor 12:12–27"),
    ("What did the ascended Christ give?", "Gifts for the work of the ministry and the edifying of the body", "Eph 4:7–16"),
    ("Are gifts and offices one list?", "No; the lists differ and Paul does not merge them", "1Cor 12; Eph 4; Rom 12"),
    ("What offices does Paul name?", "Overseer (called elder) and deacon", "Phil 1:1; 1Tim 3; Titus 1"),
    ("Are elder and bishop one office?", "Yes; Paul uses the names of the same man", "Titus 1:5–7"),
    ("What order does the meeting keep?", "Edifying, turns, interpretation, peace, decency and order", "1Cor 14:26–40"),
    ("What grounds are given for men and women?", "Headship, creation, the law, Adam first formed and Eve deceived", "1Cor 11; 14; 1Tim 2"),
    ("What is the church to do about open sin?", "Put away the sinning brother, to the stated aims; restore the repentant", "1Cor 5; 2Cor 2"),
    ("What does Paul leave unsaid?", "Much a later church order supplies", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.30, 0.46, 0.24]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says. In either case the reader will have read the passages in their places."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. Constable, Piper and Wright each read parts of these passages carefully, and each sees things the volume has used. Where one of them adds a method or an inference the sentences do not state, or draws a line the volume does not draw, the volume has said so and has answered from the text. If the volume has been unfair, the verses will show it."),
]

